  ],
  "text": "The story where money replaced bartering is in every textbook. Anthropologists have never found it. You know the story. You have eggs, I have shoes, neither of us wants the other thing. It is a tidy explanation, and no society has ever been found that actually worked that way. What turns up instead is credit. You take what you need and it gets remembered. Inside a village everyone already knows who owes whom. Nobody needs coins for that. Barter does show up, but between strangers, or when a currency has already collapsed. Which is the opposite of the story. Barter comes after money, not before it. The better explanation is that money grew out of debt records and taxes. Which is exactly what those first clay tablets are. Who owed what to whom. Money did not replace swapping goats. It came out of a bookkeeping problem. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
